markdown <<MARKDOWN
## Facilitating Building Classifiers

I don't have a confirmed teaching-guide example of students confusing "feature" and "label" specifically. What the guide does document clearly is a different, real misconception, built below.

### What's Actually Happening in This Lesson

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px; margin-bottom: 16px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">1</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Exploring Nearest Neighbors:</strong> students find Today's nearest past day by hand using the Activity Guide, using the majority label among three neighbors</div>
  </div>
  <div style="display: flex; align-items: flex-start; gap: 12px;">
    <div style="background-color: #4C42CF; color: #FFFFFF; font-weight: bold; font-size: 12px; width: 24px; height: 24px; min-width: 24px; border-radius: 6px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center;">2</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; line-height: 1.5;"><strong>Nearest Neighbors Classification (Levels 1-4):</strong> students run a program with two features, change Today's values, then change how many neighbors are considered</div>
  </div>
</div>

<hr>

### Watch for: Assuming More Neighbors Is Always Better

<div style="display: flex; gap: 16px; margin: 20px 0; flex-wrap: wrap;">
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #FFE3CE; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #611D00; margin-bottom: 10px;">Stuck: More Is Always Better</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #611D00; line-height: 1.6;">Sets the neighbor count as high as possible, assuming more data means a more reliable prediction.</div>
  </div>
  <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 250px; background-color: #CCF1D0; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 18px; box-shadow: 2px 2px 8px rgba(0,0,0,0.1);">
    <div style="font-size: 16px; font-weight: bold; color: #003F25; margin-bottom: 10px;">Strong: Tests a Range</div>
    <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #003F25; line-height: 1.6;">Tries a small number (2-3) and a large number (10+), and notices the prediction can get worse, not better, as neighbors increase.</div>
  </div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #E4E2F8; border-radius: 10px; padding: 16px 20px; margin: 16px 0;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222; line-height: 1.5;">
    <strong>&#128161; Language to borrow:</strong>
  </div>
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #222222;">&bull; "What happens to the plot as you change the number of neighbors? Does the prediction stay stable, or does it flip?"</div>
</div>

<div style="background-color: #D5EFFF; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 18px; margin-top: 20px;">
  <div style="font-size: 16px; color: #06338D; line-height: 1.6;">
    <strong>&#8505; The real mechanism:</strong> at some point, a large neighbor count starts pulling in points that aren't actually similar to Today, you lose the signal in the noise. If students are stuck, have them compare predictions at a small k versus a very large one directly.
  </div>
</div>
MARKDOWN
